      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that can be drawn inside a rectangle \\(ABCD\\) with side lengths 10 and 11, we need to consider the geometry of the problem. The largest equilateral triangle that can fit inside the rectangle will have one of its sides along one of the sides of the rectangle and its vertices touching the other two sides.\n\nLet's consider the case where the equilateral triangle has one side along the length of the rectangle (11 units) and two vertices touching the shorter side (10 units). The height of the equilateral triangle will be the altitude from the vertex opposite the base to the base of length 10.\n\nThe formula for the height \\(h\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is:\n\\[\nh = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s\n\\]\nIn this case, the side length \\(s\\) of the equilateral triangle is 10. Therefore, the height \\(h\\) is:\n\\[\nh = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times 10 = 5\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThe area \\(A\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{1}{2} s^2 \\sin \\theta\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between two sides of the triangle. For an equilateral triangle, \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\) and \\(\\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Thus, the area is:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{1}{2} s^2 \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10^2 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 100 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 25\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nTherefore, the maximum possible area of such a triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{55\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
