      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle drawn within a rectangle with sides 10 and 11, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the constraints and properties of the rectangle and the triangle:**\n   - The rectangle \\(ABCD\\) has side lengths \\(AB = 10\\) and \\(BC = 11\\).\n   - An equilateral triangle is inscribed within the rectangle such that no point of the triangle lies outside the rectangle.\n\n2. **Determine the maximum side length of the equilateral triangle:**\n   - The maximum side length of the equilateral triangle that can fit within the rectangle is limited by the shorter dimension of the rectangle, which is 10.\n   - Therefore, the side length of the equilateral triangle \\(s\\) must be less than or equal to 10.\n\n3. **Use the formula for the area of an equilateral triangle:**\n   - The area \\(A\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n     \\[\n     A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n     \\]\n\n4. **Find the maximum area:**\n   - Substitute the maximum side length \\(s = 10\\) into the area formula:\n     \\[\n     A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 10^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 100 = 25\\sqrt{3}\n     \\]\n\n5. **State the final answer:**\n   - The maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle that can fit within the rectangle is:\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{25\\sqrt{3}}\n     \\]",
