      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that can fit inside a rectangle \\(ABCD\\) with side lengths 10 and 11, we need to consider the possible positions of the equilateral triangle with respect to the rectangle.\n\nThe largest equilateral triangle that can fit inside the rectangle will have one vertex on each of the sides of the rectangle, and the other two vertices will be inside the rectangle. Let's denote the side length of the equilateral triangle by \\(s\\).\n\nThe height \\(h\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[\nh = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s\n\\]\nFor the triangle to fit inside the rectangle, the height \\(h\\) must be less than or equal to the smaller of the two side lengths of the rectangle, which is 10. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\leq 10 \\implies s \\leq \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\nThe maximum possible side length \\(s\\) of the equilateral triangle that can fit inside the rectangle is \\(\\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\).\n\nThe area \\(A\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(s = \\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) into the area formula, we get:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left(\\frac{20\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{400 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1200}{9} = \\frac{300\\sqrt{3}}{9} = \\frac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\nTherefore, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{100\\sqrt{3}}{3}}\n\\]",
