      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that can fit entirely within a rectangle with side lengths 10 and 11.\n\nAn equilateral triangle has all sides equal, so if we denote the side length of the equilateral triangle as \\( s \\), then the height of the equilateral triangle can be given by the formula:\n\\[ h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s. \\]\nThe triangle must fit entirely within the rectangle, so the side length \\( s \\) of the triangle must be less than or equal to the smaller side of the rectangle. Since the side lengths of the rectangle are 10 and 11, the maximum possible value for \\( s \\) is 10.\n\nSubstituting \\( s = 10 \\) into the height formula, we get:\n\\[ h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\times 10 = 5\\sqrt{3}. \\]\nThe area \\( A \\) of an equilateral triangle is given by the formula:\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2. \\]\nSubstituting \\( s = 10 \\) into the area formula, we get:\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 10^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 100 = 25\\sqrt{3}. \\]\nThus, the maximum possible area of such an equilateral triangle is:\n\\[ \\boxed{25\\sqrt{3}}. \\]",
